---
description: Apply when production is down or degraded, an alert is firing, or users are impacted
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Mitigate before diagnosing — users experience downtime, not root causes. Root cause comes after recovery; learning comes in the postmortem.
- Assess in two minutes: impact (who, what, since when — check the user-facing symptom yourself), severity (data corruption outranks downtime), trajectory. State it in the channel.
- Keep a timestamped append-only log from minute one; log every state-changing action before taking it — it prevents conflicting changes and becomes the postmortem's raw material.
- Mitigation logic: what changed? (deploys, config, flags, providers) → rollback of the suspect change is the default first move; correlation suffices. No candidate → shed and shield (flag off, fail over, scale up, rate-limit, serve degraded). Reversible beats clever; snapshot before touching data. One mitigation at a time, verified against the user-facing symptom; worse → revert immediately.
- Communicate on a cadence — impact in user terms, status, next update time — kept even when there's nothing new. No external cause speculation, no guessed ETAs.
- Stand down deliberately: symptom gone through one full cycle (peak, cron), follow-ups ticketed now (disabled flag, skipped root cause, snapshots), evidence preserved before it rotates, postmortem scheduled. Mitigation is not the fix — the incident ends twice. Irreversible actions get a second ack even mid-incident; two failed mitigations and guessing → escalate.
